WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.936
Java gives developers several
file-handling tools.

2
00:00:03.016 --> 00:00:05.397
Older code may use java.io.File and stream
classes.

3
00:00:05.477 --> 00:00:09.211
Modern code often uses java.nio.file.Path
and Files for filesystem operations.

4
00:00:09.291 --> 00:00:12.755
Frameworks may add resources, multipart
abstractions, download helpers,

5
00:00:12.835 --> 00:00:13.810
and storage clients.

6
00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.975
These tools can join paths, resolve
children, normalize segments,

7
00:00:17.055 --> 00:00:19.544
inspect file attributes, open streams, and
move data.

8
00:00:19.624 --> 00:00:22.006
They are useful, but they are not security
policies.

9
00:00:22.086 --> 00:00:25.658
Path APIs can support a safe design by
making path manipulation more explicit.

10
00:00:25.738 --> 00:00:29.472
For example, a service can start from an
application-controlled base directory,

11
00:00:29.552 --> 00:00:31.771
resolve a candidate name, normalize the
result,

12
00:00:31.851 --> 00:00:35.315
and then check whether the final path
remains inside the intended boundary.

13
00:00:35.395 --> 00:00:37.614
That is better than ad hoc string
concatenation.

14
00:00:37.694 --> 00:00:39.751
But the path check only answers one
question:

15
00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:42.104
whether this filesystem path is inside
this base.

16
00:00:42.184 --> 00:00:45.648
It does not answer whether the user should
access the file or whether the file

17
00:00:45.728 --> 00:00:47.135
belongs to the correct tenant.

18
00:00:47.215 --> 00:00:50.354
Filesystem behavior can vary by operating
system, filesystem type,

19
00:00:50.434 --> 00:00:54.277
case sensitivity, symbolic links,
separators, permissions, and mounted volumes.

20
00:00:54.357 --> 00:00:58.038
Containers add another layer because the
process may see a different filesystem

21
00:00:58.118 --> 00:00:59.092
layout than the host.

22
00:00:59.172 --> 00:01:01.445
Object storage uses keys rather than local
paths,

23
00:01:01.525 --> 00:01:04.448
but keys can still be misconstructed,
overbroad, predictable,

24
00:01:04.528 --> 00:01:06.205
or exposed through policy mistakes.

25
00:01:06.285 --> 00:01:09.858
A defensive design acknowledges those
differences instead of assuming every

26
00:01:09.938 --> 00:01:12.048
environment behaves like a developer
laptop.

27
00:01:12.128 --> 00:01:15.430
The safest public interface often avoids
direct path control entirely.

28
00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.404
Instead of accepting a path from the user,

29
00:01:17.484 --> 00:01:20.623
the application can accept an
application-controlled identifier,

30
00:01:20.703 --> 00:01:22.760
look up a file record, verify
authorization,

31
00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.221
and then map that record to a known
storage location.

32
00:01:25.301 --> 00:01:27.683
This separates user intent from storage
mechanics.

33
00:01:27.763 --> 00:01:31.497
normalize, resolve, canonicalization, and
real-path checks can help inside the

34
00:01:31.577 --> 00:01:34.500
implementation, but they should not be the
only line of defense.
